{"type":"api-request-blob","sessionId":"15c732d1-457b-4efc-91e5-664943f21b1c","hash":"551b996a151cbfb415b319d1ceedb1cd3ec8843da5c0a9b282d3b7a20490ba4c","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_015AyCTP69Jk1rDEL1LcUVdL","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/Users/gabe/.cargo/registry/src/index.crates.io-1949cf8c6b5b557f/michi-graph-0.7.0/CHANGELOG.md"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01PphJMGuTjQysQd8Y8TGYCP","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"grep -n \"pub fn from_\\|pub fn ready_by_edge\\|pub fn prerequisite_edges\\|pub fn ready\\b\\|pub fn ready(\" /Users/gabe/.cargo/registry/src/index.crates.io-1949cf8c6b5b557f/michi-graph-0.7.0/src/*.rs; grep -n \"fn operation_dag\" -B2 -A30 crates/callisto-graph/src/commands/release_execution.rs","description":"List michi constructors and read Callisto's operation_dag"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}]}}
{"type":"api-request-blob","sessionId":"15c732d1-457b-4efc-91e5-664943f21b1c","hash":"6b3a5f017e238b3e2f74b98713b6f401c4e4ec2b6ffe4f4553d6feaab8b2d3cc","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_015AyCTP69Jk1rDEL1LcUVdL","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# michi-graph\n2\t\n3\t## 0.7.0\n4\t\n5\t- michi-graph: a `Dag` keeps its edges' kinds. `Dag<K, E = ()>` gains `prerequisite_edges(ix)` (every kept edge to a prerequisite with its kind) and `ready_by_edge(eligible, satisfied)`, which judges each edge with its kind, for graphs where some kinds are satisfied earlier than others (a sibling publish needs a publish issued, a tag needs it confirmed). `Graph::validate` and `validate_filtered` now return `Dag<K, E>` and need `E: Clone`; code naming `Dag<K>` for graphs without kinds is unchanged.\n6\t\n7\t## 0.6.0\n8\t\n9\t- Released together with the `michi` fixed group.\n10\t\n11\t## 0.5.0\n12\t\n13\t- michi-graph: first release. An MIT, dependency-free (`no_std` + `alloc`) crate for deterministic dependency graphs: build with `GraphBuilder`, validate into a `Dag` that reports every problem at once (duplicate nodes, unknown dependencies, self-dependencies, and cycles with members and one shortest loop), then query the topological order, layers, ready sets, ancestors and descendants. Ties break by declaration order or by key, and every output follows the choice. Edge kinds and per-validation edge filters let one graph produce several dags. See `docs/spec/09-graph.md`.\n14\t- Joined the `michi` group at this version.\n15\t\n16\t"},{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01PphJMGuTjQysQd8Y8TGYCP","type":"tool_result","content":"/Users/gabe/.cargo/registry/src/index.crates.io-1949cf8c6b5b557f/michi-graph-0.7.0/src/dag.rs:68:    pub fn from_nodes<I, D>(nodes: I, order: Order) -> Result<Self, Violations<K>>\n/Users/gabe/.cargo/registry/src/index.crates.io-1949cf8c6b5b557f/michi-graph-0.7.0/src/dag.rs:203:    pub fn ready<'a, S, R>(&'a self, satisfied: S, eligible: R) -> impl Iterator<Item = NodeIx> + 'a\n/Users/gabe/.cargo/registry/src/index.crates.io-1949cf8c6b5b557f/michi-graph-0.7.0/src/dag.rs:218:    pub fn prerequisite_edges(&self, ix: NodeIx) -> &[(NodeIx, E)] {\n/Users/gabe/.cargo/registry/src/index.crates.io-1949cf8c6b5b557f/michi-graph-0.7.0/src/dag.rs:226:    pub fn ready_by_edge<'a, R, S>(&'a self, eligible: R, satisfied: S) -> impl Iterator<Item = NodeIx> + 'a\n176-/// The intent's operations as a DAG, built once per run. A constructed intent is already a proven DAG, so a failure\n177-/// here means the intent was altered after validation.\n178:pub(crate) fn operation_dag(intent: &ReleaseIntentV1) -> Result<Dag<ReleaseOperationId>, GraphError> {\n179-    Dag::from_nodes(\n180-        intent\n181-            .operations\n182-            .iter()\n183-            .map(|operation| (operation.id().clone(), operation.prerequisites().to_vec())),\n184-        Order::Key,\n185-    )\n186-    .map_err(|violations| GraphError::ReleaseInvariant {\n187-        detail: format!(\"the release operations do not form a valid DAG: {violations}\"),\n188-    })\n189-}\n190-\n191-/// The wait for issued publishes to show. It opens when the first of them is issued and stays open until none is\n192-/// outstanding, so a publish confirmed along the way does not start the window again, and it runs on the clock, so the\n193-/// time spent looking counts. The looks keep their schedule from one settle to the next.\n194-struct ConfirmationWave {\n195-    opened: std::time::Instant,\n196-    looks: u32,\n197-}\n198-\n199-impl ConfirmationWave {\n200-    fn open(now: std::time::Instant) -> Self {\n201-        ConfirmationWave { opened: now, looks: 0 }\n202-    }\n203-}\n204-\n205-/// Looks at every issued operation until one is shown, so the run goes on with whatever that unlocks, or gives up\n206-/// once the registries have had their whole window.\n207-///\n208-/// Each look is one observation per issued operation, and the issued operations are looked at together: the wait is","is_error":false}]}}
