{"parentUuid":"a8611b46-2572-4f07-8c29-6b8011a9197e","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011CgMTT5q8bcabAACoMN5Yo","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01EKjNk6rWzDouhwafBBJC3x","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"Adversarial: failure and recovery (retry)","subagent_type":"bug-hunter","prompt":"You are a principal engineer doing an ADVERSARIAL review of \"Callisto\", a Rust CLI + GitHub Action that versions and releases polyglot workspaces (Cargo/npm/PyPI) from changesets. Repo: /Users/gabe/Projects/callisto/.claude/worktrees/smoke-main (main, v0.14.2). Read ARCHITECTURE.md, AGENTS.md, docs/adr/ (esp. 2, 3, 9, 12), docs/architecture/release.md and docs/specs/release.json first. Product goal: releases are irreversible effects on public registries; the tool must be safe to crash, rerun, run concurrently, and run on a flaky network, without publishing wrong code, tagging the wrong commit, or wedging a release.\n\nA previous attempt at this review died from an API error; do not rely on it. Work efficiently and write your final report EARLY: after each 3-4 findings, keep notes so a crash loses little. Aim to finish within about 40 tool calls.\n\nRULES: read-only. Do NOT edit/commit/push, do NOT run cargo build/test/publish/clippy, no network publishes. You may read, grep, and run the prebuilt binary /Users/gabe/.claude/jobs/15c732d1/tmp/profile-target/release/callisto on scratch repos under a UNIQUE directory `$TMPDIR/adv-recovery2-$$` (other agents may run in parallel; never use a shared path, never rm outside your own dir). The worktree guard refuses git commands run directly; build scratch repos with a small Python helper (subprocess to /usr/bin/git) or a script file. A fake `cargo`/`npm` on PATH inside your scratch dir is fine for exercising release flows locally (see crates/callisto-cli/tests/common/release_harness.rs for the pattern). Never run git commands in the real repo except read-only `git log/show`.\n\nYOUR LENS: failure, recovery and concurrency semantics. Walk every effect (registry publish, tag create+push, GitHub release draft/upload/publish, release-PR commit) and ask: what if the process dies right before / right after / during it; what if the registry shows the version late, shows a DIFFERENT artifact, rate-limits, or 5xx; what if two runs overlap (CI re-run, two merges, local + CI); what if HEAD moves between plan and execute; what if a tag exists remotely but not locally or points elsewhere; what if the registry accepts the publish but the response is lost (non-idempotent effect with unknown outcome); partial multi-package/multi-registry releases and how a rerun adopts them (ADR 3); the E320 version-commit logic (crates/callisto-graph/src/commands/release/version_commit.rs) including the case \"published from commit F1, tag step failed, another commit F2 lands, rerun\"; the confirmation window (release_execution.rs); fixed-group and dual-ecosystem packages; Python multi-file uploads; npm dist-tag outcomes (stable release moving `latest` backwards); receipts. Find states that are unrecoverable, silently wrong, or that wedge a rerun with a misleading error.\nFor each finding give: severity, exact file:line, the concrete interleaving/scenario, whether you REPRODUCED it (command + output) or only READ it, and a one-line fix direction. Rank by severity. At most 10 findings; drop speculation you cannot tie to code. Also list 3 things done WELL. Be concise and precise."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1070,"cache_read_input_tokens":571372,"output_tokens":1622,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":252},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1070,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"global","ite