{"parentUuid":"ee95dc2d-a5f1-463b-97df-aceafa266c39","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011CgMU7YWKBqJRMMEtiK6bF","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"","signature":""}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"end_turn","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":5650,"cache_read_input_tokens":582877,"output_tokens":5902,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":2802},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":5650,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"global","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":5902,"cache_read_input_tokens":582877,"cache_creation_input_tokens":5650,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":5650},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"thinkingDurationMs":24756,"thinkingDisplay":"updates","serverClassifierRequest":"44128406-d462-4f5b-ac54-76b1e87a067b","apiBlockIndex":0,"requestId":"req_011CgMU7XhwmqmUiWnBmPxfe","requestRef":"a6c7425d-cacd-4b5b-8ea1-2a9721f2a939","requestedModel":"claude-sonnet-5-5","type":"assistant","uuid":"452b8527-8f7a-4b9c-9a65-0861fc185eb5","timestamp":"2026-10-24T17:37:45.486Z","advisorModel":"claude-opus-5-5","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","session_id":"15c732d1-457b-4efc-91e5-664943f21b1c","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/gabe/Projects/callisto/.claude/worktrees/smoke-main","sessionId":"15c732d1-457b-4efc-91e5-664943f21b1c","version":"2.1.304","gitBranch":"chore/release-target-facts","slug":"lexical-jingling-puppy"}
{"parentUuid":"452b8527-8f7a-4b9c-9a65-0861fc185eb5","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011CgMU7YWKBqJRMMEtiK6bF","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"# Adversarial review, revised for a ship-first v1\n\n## The triage rule\n\nA finding blocks v1 only if it passes one of three tests:\n- It produces a **wrong result silently**, such as a wrong version, a wrong tag, or a publish to the wrong place.\n- It is a **one-way door**, meaning something users or tools will depend on and we can't change later without a break.\n- It is **cheap**, under about a day.\n\nEverything else gets documented, filed, and fixed when real use shows it matters. The earlier report listed about 40 findings. Applying this rule, **8 are v1 gates, 9 are cheap soon-after fixes, and most of the rest we ship with.** I also say below where the first report over-reached.\n\n## v1 gates (do now, each small)\n\n| # | Fix | Size | Why it can't wait |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | **Unresolved changeset entry re-bumps every run.** Make an unresolved entry a hard error for the whole run. | S | Silent wrong versions, and it's reproduced. |\n| 2 | **Skip non-publishable and version-less members** (an xtask with `publish = false`, a dynamic-version pyproject) with a note, unless a changeset names them. | S | This is a first-hour failure for almost every Rust workspace. It hurts the \"better than current tools\" goal more than any security finding. |\n| 3 | **Release-commit path allow-list.** Reject a release commit that touches anything outside manifests, changelogs, lockfiles, the decision file, changesets and `pre.json`. | S–M | The whole trust model rests on \"the diff is the decision\". Today the diff can carry anything. A path allow-list is enough; re-running `version` on the parent would be over-engineering. |\n| 4 | **Pass the built-in registry URL explicitly** to `cargo publish` and `npm publish`. | S | A one-flag fix that closes the \"committed `.npmrc` redirects the token\" path. Don't ignore the project `.npmrc`, because users rely on it for scoped registries. |\n| 5 | **Warn when a version is tagged locally but the tag isn't on `origin`.** A diagnostic in `release` and `status`, nothing more. | S | This makes the silent \"Nothing to release\" abandonment visible. The full resume logic can wait. |\n| 6 | **Delete the unsafe installer fallback to `main` HEAD, and pass `--source-ref` to attestation verify.** | S | This removes code, and it keeps untrusted code out of the credentialed job. |\n| 7 | **Add the three missing credential names to the scrub list** (`CARGO_REGISTRIES_*_TOKEN`, `ACTIONS_ID_TOKEN_REQUEST_*`, `UV_PUBLISH_TOKEN`). | XS | Minutes of work. |\n| 8 | **Write a one-page stability promise** and settle ADR 12. | S | The one-way doors are about naming what's stable, not about building anything. |\n\n## Soon after v1 (cheap; do when convenient)\n\n- npm ranges with spaces or a hyphen: warn, and later parse them properly.\n- Peer ranges: skip `Peer` in the co-release floor raise.\n- Tighten \"already published\" detection to require a non-zero exit and a structured match.\n- Improve the misleading E121 message when the first `version` run simply hasn't been committed.\n- `package.json` writes: keep inline arrays and number text intact. Hand-formatted `\"files\": [...]` expanding in every release PR is visible noise, so this is the first item here that could cost us users.\n- BOM changelog duplicate heading, and release notes cut at an indented `# `.\n- Poetry rewrites dropping comments, and Python `~=` precision.\n- `snapshot --tag` validation, and `git add -A` in the release-PR script.\n- Tests that pay for themselves:\n  - make the actionlint and zizmor skip fail under `CI`;\n  - add job `timeout-minutes`;\n  - add a handful of `git_batch.rs` fail-closed tests;\n  - add pnpm/yarn/bun to the real-tool flag contract.\n\n## Ship, document, wait for signal\n\nThese are real, but rare or already mitigated. A known-limits note is enough, and we fix them when someone hits one.\n- **Dist-tag `latest` moving backwards** on a backport. `npm-tag` is already configurable per registry, so document it.\n- **E320 equivalence** that compares only the package directory, and **an untagged published version** orphaned by a later bump.\n- **Persisted git token in `execute`.** The registry token is already in that job, and it is the more valuable one. Restructuring the job is real work for marginal gain, so document it.\n- Concurrent-run edge cases: queue replacement, duplicate drafts, half-uploaded assets, mixed Python builds.\n- Unlocked `pip`/`npx` installs in the generated matrix workflow, and unquoted branch names in YAML.\n- Dual-package ownership priority, and the consumer trust model being simpler than this repo's own workflow. State it plainly.\n- Exit codes: keep 0 for success, 1 for failure and 2 for usage. Adding more later is compatible.\n\n## What we are over-thinking or over-engineering\n\n1. **Perf work beyond batching.** The speedup was entirely from batching (50–115× since 0.12). The hints were reverted for this reason. Stop.\n2. **The michi graph and pipeline adoption** (`ready_by_edge`, `michi-pipeline`). I dropped `ready_by_edge` because it would add code and keep the check it can't replace. Don't revisit.\n3. **Transactional two-phase apply.** Git is the transaction. A half-written tree is a `git checkout` away, so fixing the E121 message is enough.\n4. **Per-tool environment allowlists and a credential-scrubbing redesign.** Extend the denylist (item 7) and move on.\n5. **Keyed or signed decision digests** and **making consumers' workflows match this repo's three-job form.** Document the simpler model.\n6. **Spec-to-test mapping infrastructure.** I just did the drift pass by hand, and it found about 20 fixes in 249 criteria. Repeat it before each major release, not with tooling.\n7. **Coverage-gate redesign, running fuzz in CI, and de-opaquing snapshots.** Real but not v1 work.\n8. **Pre-building for GitLab, Go and NuGet.** The right architecture is already there: one adapter trait per ecosystem and a non-exhaustive `Ecosystem`. A second forge is an intent version bump when someone needs it, so say \"GitHub + Cargo/npm/PyPI\" and build nothing ahead of that.\n9. **Tag-name redesign.** The honest options are *keep and document* or *always qualify*. The second changes tags for current users. I'd keep the default, document that adding a same-named package changes new tags, and tell people to pin `tag-template`. That's a docs decision for you, not engineering.\n10. **A frozen error taxonomy.** Error codes are already stable per code, and consolidating duplicates (E317 vs E227) can happen when it bothers someone.\n\nThe first report also over-weighted its security findings. Several attack paths (#3–#5) need someone who can already write to the repo or its dependencies, and were never run. That's why they're a small allow-list, a one-flag fix and a documented limit, not a hardening project.\n\n## Delete before v1 (negative code)\n\n- The `[init]` table, `release.profiles`, and the no-op `setup_git_user` input.\n- `nuget` as an accepted `publish-to` value with no publisher.\n- The installer's `cargo install --git main` fallback.\n- Unused `Ecosystem` variants only if they show up in public docs; otherwise leave them.\n\nEvery legacy key kept becomes a 1.0 promise, so removing them now is cheaper than breaking them later.\n\n## Why the architecture holds for growth\n\n- **Adding a language** means one registry adapter, one manifest editor and a few table entries. The decision, intent and recovery model doesn't change.\n- **The identity model** (directory-keyed, display-promoted, canonical `ecosystem/name`) supports more ecosystems without redesign. The only open risk is the tag-name behavior above.\n- **Hidden commands and report schemas** are the real growth constraint, so the stability page should name which ones are supported.\n\n## Decisions for you\n\n1. Tag naming: keep and document, or always qualify.\n2. Settle ADR 12 (accept as written, or trim).\n3. Whether non-publishable or version-less members are skipped by default (my recommendation) or need an explicit setting.\n\n## Revised sequence\n\nFour small PRs, then v1 candidate:\n1. Changeset hard-error, plus skipping non-publishable members (#1, #2).\n2. Release-commit allow-list, explicit registry URL, credential names, remove the installer fallback (#3, #4, #6, #7).\n3. Diagnostic for a local-only tag (#5), plus the cheap tests.\n4. Stability page, ADR 12, deleting the legacy keys (#8).\n\nThen run the real-registry end-to-end with your credentials, since that's still the biggest unverified step. Do you want me to start with PR 1?"}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"end_turn","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":5650,"cache_read_input_tokens":582877,"output_tokens":5902,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":2802},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":5650,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"global","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":5902,"cache_read_input_tokens":582877,"cache_creation_input_tokens":5650,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":5650},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"serverClassifierRequest":"44128406-d462-4f5b-ac54-76b1e87a067b","apiBlockIndex":1,"requestId":"req_011CgMU7XhwmqmUiWnBmPxfe","requestRef":"a6c7425d-cacd-4b5b-8ea1-2a9721f2a939","requestedModel":"claude-sonnet-5-5","type":"assistant","uuid":"793baf5f-67a2-4a9a-97ef-36af4f674744","timestamp":"2026-10-24T17:38:09.079Z","advisorModel":"claude-opus-5-5","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","session_id":"15c732d1-457b-4efc-91e5-664943f21b1c","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/gabe/Projects/callisto/.claude/worktrees/smoke-main","sessionId":"15c732d1-457b-4efc-91e5-664943f21b1c","version":"2.1.304","gitBranch":"chore/release-target-facts","slug":"lexical-jingling-puppy"}
